00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:05.360
So a little bit of
background on me--

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.660
so I joined Google
about two years ago.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:11.117
Before that, I was working
for a company called Apigee.

00:01:11.117 --> 00:01:12.700
A lot of you may
have heard of Apigee.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.670
We're an API
management platform.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.220
We got bought by Google.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:18.130
And so for the last
four or so years,

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:21.490
I've been spending almost all
my time talking with customers,

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:23.410
different companies
about the API programs

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:25.285
that they're creating,
the challenges they're

00:01:25.285 --> 00:01:26.860
running into,
trying to figure out

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:29.020
how it is that we can
build a platform that's

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:33.040
going to make our customers
the most successful as we can.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.080
And before that, I
was interested in APIs

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.240
because I was building
a lot of APIs.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.450
And when I first started
getting into APIs,

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:45.010
there was a big debate raging
in industry between things like,

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.320
should we use SOAP,
or should we use REST?

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:51.910
And at the time, it seemed
like SOAP had basically won.

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:55.810
Enterprises were viewing APIs
as this novelty technology

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:58.152
for the Facebooks and
Twitters of the world and not

00:01:58.152 --> 00:01:59.860
really something that
serious enterprises

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:05.417
would need or be building
to satisfy their use cases.

00:02:05.417 --> 00:02:07.000
And then, a couple
of things happened.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.400
We had things like
big improvements

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.450
in the way that websites worked.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.570
So we went from
pages that needed

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:15.760
to reload every time to
single-page apps that

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.340
are much more responsive.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.090
And if you look at
frameworks like Angular

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.640
and others that have
come out, they natively

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:22.621
consumed this JSON.

00:02:22.621 --> 00:02:24.370
And so all of a sudden,
we had enterprises

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:28.259
building APIs to support
these new types of websites.

00:02:28.259 --> 00:02:29.800
And then of course,
at the same time,

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:33.340
mobile just became the
explosion that it became.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:37.280
And as a result, the same
type of thing applied.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:39.220
So if you've ever
tried to consume a SOAP

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.975
service from, say, like an
iOS app or building an iOS

00:02:41.975 --> 00:02:45.100
app that uses SOAP, it's a
pretty challenging exercise.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:48.209
It's pretty trivial if
you're using a RESTful API.

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:49.750
And so we had a lot
of companies that

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:53.380
were starting to build more,
and more, and more APIs.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.990
And so it's great
that today, we're

00:02:55.990 --> 00:03:00.490
sitting here in this completely
filled room talking about APIs,

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:02.470
and somewhat
surprising that SOAP--

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:04.960
even though it was
the de-facto winner--

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:07.210
I'm pretty surprised if
we find a SOAP session

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:09.670
how to build better SOAP
services on App Engine

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:12.257
or how to optimize your
Kubernetes cluster for SOAP.

00:03:12.257 --> 00:03:13.840
But we've got a lot
of people that are

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.270
interested in API management.

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:19.994
So a lot of you
are probably like,

00:03:19.994 --> 00:03:21.910
why are you talking about
the history of APIs?

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:23.451
What's that have to
do with anything?

00:03:23.451 --> 00:03:25.330
I came to a best practices talk.

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:29.770
So the reason why I like to
start this talk by describing

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:35.950
a little historical context
is that the circumstances that

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:39.970
happened around web and mobile
led to a bunch of API programs

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:41.630
springing up just
out of necessity,

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:43.180
without a lot of foresight.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.640
And this gives us the
ability to go and look

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:48.070
at a lot of common
problems that have happened

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:51.260
from these programs, which
really had no management

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:52.510
upfront.

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:57.100
And so one of the patterns that
I observed as I go and talk

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:00.220
to a lot of enterprise customers
who build out a lot of APIs,

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:02.854
is that they don't really set
out to build an API program.

00:04:02.854 --> 00:04:04.270
Usually, what
happens is something

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:08.374
more like this, where a project
just happens to come along.

00:04:08.374 --> 00:04:10.540
Maybe we're going to build
out something in Angular.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.930
Somebody says, let's build a
new customer-facing website.

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:15.810
Let's build something
for our call center.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:17.060
Great, we're going to do that.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:21.010
And it just so happens that
this application needs an API.

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:21.610
We launch it.

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:24.280
Everything is going
according to plan--

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.910
so far so good-- until a
whole bunch of other projects

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:28.930
come along.

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:31.550
And each one of those projects
needs to create APIs as well.

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:32.530
So we end up with mobile apps.

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:34.000
We end up with
self-service kiosk.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.630
We end up with all these
things that are needing APIs.

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:40.660
And so then, we start to run
into some of the challenges,

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:44.170
and we start to see the
need for API management.

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:46.750
So if you start looking
across these APIs,

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:49.330
you'll see that they're really
not necessarily consistent.

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:51.070
You'll see things
like, maybe I've

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:53.560
got a preferred approach for
doing things like pagination.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:55.740
Maybe I like to
use limited offset.

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:59.260
Maybe you like to use
page size and page number.

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:02.590
Maybe somebody else likes to
use a current set of records.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:05.500
Maybe I like to use customer
uppercase I, uppercase D.

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:09.840
And you like customer uppercase
i, lowercase d, or camel cases,

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.610
or all these different
permutations.

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:15.240
And so what ends up happening
is that as a developer,

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.634
as you go to try
to use these APIs,

00:05:17.634 --> 00:05:19.800
you're having to learn each
one over and over again.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.720
We have more
friction than we need

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:25.620
to have as we're trying to
build API-driven applications

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:28.460
in our organization.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:30.200
The other thing
that happens is, we

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:33.480
tend to run into a lot of
the same type of challenges.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.812
So if you have 10 different
teams that are building APIs,

00:05:36.812 --> 00:05:39.020
you may have 10 different
teams that are building out

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:41.330
solutions for
things like caching,

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:45.460
like traffic management,
quotas, security, and so forth.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:48.410
And so not only are
these APIs inconsistent,

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:49.970
but we're spending
a lot more effort

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:52.290
than we need to to actually
get them up and running

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:54.860
and out the door.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:59.150
And then, probably worst of all
is that because these APIs are

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:02.240
hiding in silos within
your organization,

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:03.840
they can be hard to find.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:09.531
And so we end up with APIs that
ultimately do their purpose.

00:06:09.531 --> 00:06:11.780
But we don't get a lot of
the re-usability aspects out

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:12.279
of them.

00:06:12.279 --> 00:06:14.750
We don't get a lot of
the streamline benefits,

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:17.100
and people aren't
able to find them.

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:19.040
And usually, it's
about this time

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:22.040
when companies
recognize we should

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:23.720
get more serious
about API management

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:26.570
and how we're going
to manage our APIs.

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:28.910
And so that's when
they typically

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:31.580
start to look at
platforms like Apigee

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:33.590
and start to think,
what exactly is it

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:35.330
that we should be evaluating?

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:38.360
And what capabilities should
we have in an API management

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:40.040
platform?

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:42.010
So let's talk a
little bit about that.

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:47.410
So if currently you don't have
an API management platform,

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:49.690
you've got something
homegrown, or you're just

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:52.120
evaluating what
sorts of capabilities

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:56.560
should we have to be able to
build an effective API program,

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:58.240
you'll start to see
things like this.

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.970
So we want to have
an API catalog.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:02.130
So discovery is important.

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:03.850
We want people to be
able to find APIs.

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:06.000
We want people to be
able to use those APIs.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.750
And we want to start
thinking about things

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:09.730
like the different
audiences that we

00:07:09.730 --> 00:07:11.547
might have for those APIs.

00:07:11.547 --> 00:07:13.630
So we want to make sure
our platform can do things

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:18.060
like support internal
APIs and partner APIs.

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:20.560
Maybe when somebody signs in,
we want to know which audience

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.518
they're in, and we want
to be able to make sure

00:07:22.518 --> 00:07:26.380
that we can put in place
role-based access control

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:28.810
to ensure that only the right
people should have access

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:30.580
to those APIs.

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:32.930
Depending on the types of
things that you're building,

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:34.930
you might want to have
self-service on-boarding.

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:37.810
So I know in companies
that I worked

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:40.480
for, a lot of times the
hardest part of re-use

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:42.850
was just being able
to track down the API

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:47.514
and talk to the right team to
actually get access to those.

00:07:47.514 --> 00:07:49.930
One of the things that API
management can do for you is it

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:51.850
can streamline that
entire process.

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:53.170
So we can find the APIs.

00:07:53.170 --> 00:07:55.090
They can give you a
way to get credentials

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:56.548
the same way you
would expect to be

00:07:56.548 --> 00:07:59.160
able to go to Facebook or
Twitter, sign up for their API,

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:00.410
and start using it right away.

00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:01.868
There's no reason
why you shouldn't

00:08:01.868 --> 00:08:04.257
be able to do that within
your organization as well.

00:08:04.257 --> 00:08:06.340
And of course, you should
have good documentation.

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:08.080
So this can, of
course, be things

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:12.700
like if you're using open API
for documenting or specifying

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:14.062
your API structure.

00:08:14.062 --> 00:08:15.520
We want to be able
to pull that in.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:17.710
But you may want to have
additional things as well.

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:19.990
You might want to have longer
form documentation that

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:24.250
describes, here's how you get a
OAuth token in our API program.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:29.219
Or here's how we expect
you to secure this API.

00:08:29.219 --> 00:08:30.760
Maybe you want to
put things in place

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:33.674
like SDKs or additional files
that people can download.

00:08:33.674 --> 00:08:35.049
These are all
things that you can

00:08:35.049 --> 00:08:38.440
start to think about as you
are looking at the capabilities

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:43.499
that you want from an API
portal type experience.

00:08:43.499 --> 00:08:45.040
Another thing you
want to think about

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:48.280
is analytics and how you're
going to track things, what

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:49.950
you need to track in your API.

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:52.450
So of course, you're going to
have things like, you probably

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:54.070
want to know if
all of a sudden you

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:56.560
see a big uptick in errors
that are coming through

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:58.690
or a big drop off or
a spike in traffic,

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:01.084
if things start taking
longer than you expected.

00:09:01.084 --> 00:09:02.500
Those are all
things that you want

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:03.784
to be able to put in place.

00:09:03.784 --> 00:09:05.950
And you probably want to
be able to measure business

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:07.940
impact to your APIs as well.

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:11.050
So in Apigee, we like to talk
a lot about APIs as a product

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:13.540
and taking your APIs
and productizing those,

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:18.100
so that you can expose
those to certain audiences.

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:19.870
These can be things
that are internal.

00:09:19.870 --> 00:09:22.510
Maybe you've got products that
represent a line of business,

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:24.635
and you want to be able to
offer those capabilities

00:09:24.635 --> 00:09:27.100
to other lines of business
within your organization.

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:30.040
Or you might want to productize
for an external audience.

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:32.400
Maybe you've built
things internally,

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:34.150
but they've got some
sensitive information

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:35.200
that you want to
create something

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:36.200
that's more partner-specific.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:38.033
And you don't necessarily
want to go rewrite

00:09:38.033 --> 00:09:39.477
that API from the ground up.

00:09:39.477 --> 00:09:41.810
We want to be able to look
at things like productization

00:09:41.810 --> 00:09:43.570
to help with that
type of problem.

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:45.790
And you may even want to
tie that to monetization.

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:47.740
So a lot of Apigee's
customers actually

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:52.360
have taken their API, the
data they gained over time,

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:55.930
and turned those into
monetized products

00:09:55.930 --> 00:10:00.620
that they offer to the public or
to specific business partners.

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:04.004
So that's from a
consumption standpoint,

00:10:04.004 --> 00:10:05.920
but we still want to
have all the capabilities

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:08.570
that we think about typically
with an API gateway.

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:11.900
So for example, we want to
have in place access control.

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:15.030
Now, you might be looking
at things like OAuth

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:17.290
as a standard for API security.

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:19.450
But you may need to tie
into existing systems

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:21.250
in your organization currently.

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:24.820
Maybe you use Ping for
identity access management.

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:28.030
Maybe use LDAP, or Active
Directory, other products

00:10:28.030 --> 00:10:29.240
that are out there.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:32.320
You want to make sure that you
can bridge that gap between API

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:34.720
security and the things
that you have to work

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:36.354
with in your organization.

00:10:36.354 --> 00:10:38.020
Likewise, you want
to think about things

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:39.820
like transformation
and mediation.

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:43.000
So these are capabilities
where perhaps,

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:44.911
if we have all those
inconsistent APIs

00:10:44.911 --> 00:10:47.410
that people have built, and we
don't want to go rewrite them

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:49.600
from the ground up, you
can put an API management

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:50.630
platform in place.

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:53.590
And maybe we can homogenize
some of those query parameters

00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:56.299
that people have built. We
can offer the same uniform

00:10:56.299 --> 00:10:58.840
experience to developers without
actually going and rewriting

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:01.222
everything from the ground up.

00:11:01.222 --> 00:11:02.680
You probably want
to start thinking

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:06.040
about things like traffic
management and quotas.

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:10.900
So I know I did not have a
sufficient appreciation for how

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:13.810
complex quotas could be
when I joined Apigee.

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:16.620
It actually seemed like
a pretty simple problem.

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:18.850
I give you 10 calls, and
I'm going to increment

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:20.800
a counter until it gets to 10.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:23.350
And at 11, I'm going to
stop giving you access.

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:25.840
What's so hard about that?

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:28.540
And then, I was introduced
to extremely high volumes

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:29.560
of API traffic.

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:32.710
And so if you start
getting things like 5,

00:11:32.710 --> 00:11:36.400
10, 15,000 requests per
second, keeping a quota in sync

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:37.637
can become a challenge.

00:11:37.637 --> 00:11:39.220
And then, if that
quota is distributed

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:41.800
across multiple geographies,
it becomes a really

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:43.469
non-trivial problem.

00:11:43.469 --> 00:11:45.760
So these are some of the
things you want to think about

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:47.950
as you're looking at
these gateway type

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:50.954
capabilities in API management.

00:11:50.954 --> 00:11:53.120
Of course, we have to think
about threat protection.

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:55.690
Any time that we go and we
want to expose our data out

00:11:55.690 --> 00:11:57.107
to the internet
especially, you're

00:11:57.107 --> 00:11:58.690
going to want to
make sure that you're

00:11:58.690 --> 00:12:00.340
thinking about
things like, are we

00:12:00.340 --> 00:12:04.090
protecting against DDoS attacks?

00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:05.785
Or are we protecting
against people

00:12:05.785 --> 00:12:07.285
trying to put in
place SQL injection

00:12:07.285 --> 00:12:09.820
and cross-site scripting,
those type of attacks?

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:11.890
So do we have the
capability in our platform

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:13.970
to be able to address those?

00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:16.850
And then, things like
caching and optimization--

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:18.520
so a lot of times,
we built services

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:20.730
that maybe have been
around for a long time,

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:23.740
back before we had a
lot of mobile use cases

00:12:23.740 --> 00:12:26.445
where customers expected a
very quick response time.

00:12:26.445 --> 00:12:27.820
So there are
opportunities for us

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:31.030
to build and use
caching in our platform

00:12:31.030 --> 00:12:33.010
to improve that
overall performance

00:12:33.010 --> 00:12:34.960
and experience
that we can deliver

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:36.100
through API-driven apps.

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:41.969
And so as you go through
this type of an exercise--

00:12:41.969 --> 00:12:43.760
I'm sure many of you
gone through this type

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:45.470
of an exercise--

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:47.300
typically, you end up
making a selection.

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:49.549
You decide what platform is
going to work best for you

00:12:49.549 --> 00:12:51.170
based on your use cases.

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:54.187
And it's about that
time that you realize,

00:12:54.187 --> 00:12:56.270
now you almost have two
problems because you still

00:12:56.270 --> 00:12:58.970
have all of the API
management concerns

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:01.890
that you have from all
the APIs that you've built

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:03.530
and all those type of problems.

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:04.495
And then, you have
this new platform.

00:13:04.495 --> 00:13:06.203
You have to figure
out how exactly are we

00:13:06.203 --> 00:13:11.390
going to configure this to
build a successful API program.

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:22.100
And so just like
managing anything else,

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:25.190
when we go to manage APIs, we
really have to ask ourselves,

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:26.967
what does success look like?

00:13:26.967 --> 00:13:28.550
And how are we going
to track, and how

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:30.258
are we going to measure
success over time

00:13:30.258 --> 00:13:33.660
to make sure we're on
target to achieve our goals?

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:37.940
So in talking with
a lot of customers,

00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:40.059
I've asked so many
customers, what exactly

00:13:40.059 --> 00:13:42.350
do you envision when you
think of an API program that's

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:42.980
successful?

00:13:42.980 --> 00:13:44.550
How would you measure that?

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:46.200
How would you think about that?

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:49.190
And I've heard of a number
of common themes over time.

00:13:49.190 --> 00:13:50.960
And I've tried to
categorize these.

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:54.800
So one of the first questions
that new customers want to ask

00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:57.200
is, where should we start?

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:59.000
We've built a bunch of APIs.

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:02.180
Should we just take every API,
every service we've built,

00:14:02.180 --> 00:14:06.120
dump it out on our dev portal,
let people start using that?

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:08.240
And that's one approach.

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:11.010
It's usually not the
most successful approach.

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:13.160
Where I recommend
that customers start

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:15.770
is by looking at the
end user, and what's

00:14:15.770 --> 00:14:18.270
going to provide value
to those end users.

00:14:18.270 --> 00:14:19.580
So I'll give you an example.

00:14:19.580 --> 00:14:21.740
So I worked with
a large retailer.

00:14:21.740 --> 00:14:24.800
And so one of the first
APIs that they started with,

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:28.430
that they really started
to publish and market,

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:30.500
was a loyalty program.

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:34.310
And so we talked about how
they were measuring the value.

00:14:34.310 --> 00:14:36.050
How do you know
if you're actually

00:14:36.050 --> 00:14:38.220
reaching these end users?

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:40.240
And so they did a
couple of things.

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:42.961
So one is, they started to
look at traffic for the loyalty

00:14:42.961 --> 00:14:43.460
program.

00:14:43.460 --> 00:14:45.890
How many people were actually
coming into their store

00:14:45.890 --> 00:14:48.950
because they had loyalty points
and redeeming those points?

00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:50.480
They started to look.

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:52.700
They got some early
signals by API traffic.

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:54.920
So as they rolled this out
to partners and directly

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:56.337
to consumers, they
started to see,

00:14:56.337 --> 00:14:58.336
yup, we're actually getting
a pretty good uptick

00:14:58.336 --> 00:15:01.060
in terms of people that are
taking advantage of our loyalty

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:01.560
program.

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:05.120
And it's driving those customers
back to the store more.

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:08.120
And they can watch
that usage in real time

00:15:08.120 --> 00:15:11.120
on a day-to-day basis.

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:15.550
The next aspect, once you have
identified that these APIs are

00:15:15.550 --> 00:15:17.890
going to allow us to provide
value to the end user,

00:15:17.890 --> 00:15:20.765
is to convince developers
to start using those APIs.

00:15:20.765 --> 00:15:23.140
Now, for an internal program,
that's not necessarily hard

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:25.390
because we've got a
little bit more control

00:15:25.390 --> 00:15:28.000
over what our internal
developers are

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:29.840
building on a day-to-day basis.

00:15:29.840 --> 00:15:33.130
But as you're looking at
public-facing APIs, or partner

00:15:33.130 --> 00:15:34.732
APIs where you're
recruiting people,

00:15:34.732 --> 00:15:36.940
you want to make sure that
you are offering something

00:15:36.940 --> 00:15:38.273
to those application developers.

00:15:38.273 --> 00:15:42.100
And that could be in the
form of a better experience,

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:43.510
new capabilities in their apps.

00:15:43.510 --> 00:15:47.950
It could be in the form of a
direct monetization channel.

00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:50.830
We've got retailers that are
doing things like revenue

00:15:50.830 --> 00:15:51.964
sharing monetization.

00:15:51.964 --> 00:15:53.380
So when you sign
up for their API,

00:15:53.380 --> 00:15:56.305
you can actually
drive monetization

00:15:56.305 --> 00:15:58.934
to be your application.

00:15:58.934 --> 00:16:01.100
And then, when we look at
how customers are actually

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:03.350
measuring this, one
of the most common

00:16:03.350 --> 00:16:06.150
is actually looking at the
number of consuming apps.

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:09.420
So if we've been able to reach
the audience that we're after,

00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:11.780
we get people to be
aware of those APIs.

00:16:11.780 --> 00:16:14.450
So they're actually starting
to use those and build those.

00:16:14.450 --> 00:16:17.180
Have we built an API
that's usable by developers

00:16:17.180 --> 00:16:18.831
and offers sufficient value?

00:16:18.831 --> 00:16:21.080
We'll also see the number
of developers that are using

00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:23.930
those APIs go up substantially.

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:27.540
And we can also watch and see
how our community is growing.

00:16:27.540 --> 00:16:30.424
Do we see more questions
either directly on our portal?

00:16:30.424 --> 00:16:32.090
Are we using things
like Stack Overflow?

00:16:32.090 --> 00:16:34.600
We see more interests
that are climbing up.

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:37.550
And we can measure these, and
we can take appropriate steps

00:16:37.550 --> 00:16:39.980
to continue to encourage
that type of growth.

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:46.770
The third thing is that the
APIs, as they're being used,

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:50.389
they generate some type of
ROI for your organization.

00:16:50.389 --> 00:16:52.430
And this could be in a
number of different forms.

00:16:52.430 --> 00:16:54.950
This could be from
enabling more partners

00:16:54.950 --> 00:16:57.445
to on-board on to your API,
growing your partner channels

00:16:57.445 --> 00:16:59.570
and the number of people
who can sell your products

00:16:59.570 --> 00:17:00.980
or use your data.

00:17:00.980 --> 00:17:02.420
It can be in the
form, especially

00:17:02.420 --> 00:17:05.750
for internal programs, of just
greater internal efficiency.

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:07.400
And there's a lot of
time that's wasted

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:10.520
by people trying to figure
out does a certain API exist?

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:12.920
What team do I need to talk
to get access to an API

00:17:12.920 --> 00:17:15.450
once I determine
that it does exist?

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:19.310
Is there a way that we can drive
efficiency from that aspect?

00:17:19.310 --> 00:17:23.119
Also, from the aspect of as
people are building those APIs,

00:17:23.119 --> 00:17:28.130
how can we make our
applications get built faster?

00:17:28.130 --> 00:17:29.570
And then, we can
also, of course,

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:32.480
sometimes just take the APIs
directly and monetize those.

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:34.300
So we've got
customers out there,

00:17:34.300 --> 00:17:37.930
like a large weather
site, they'll

00:17:37.930 --> 00:17:41.690
offer you subscription bases for
getting access to their weather

00:17:41.690 --> 00:17:43.010
data, for example.

00:17:43.010 --> 00:17:46.340
Others that have market
data or accessing things

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:48.900
like credit card processing.

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:51.730
So there are ways that we
can also look at these APIs

00:17:51.730 --> 00:17:55.568
as a direct new channel of
business for our organizations.

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:02.060
So when we look
across the end-to-end,

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:04.430
going from that end user
that's using the application,

00:18:04.430 --> 00:18:07.700
making sure that we're driving
the ability for the developers

00:18:07.700 --> 00:18:12.080
to get those apps up and running
more quickly to use those APIs

00:18:12.080 --> 00:18:15.740
and to ensure that we're
driving the overall ROI,

00:18:15.740 --> 00:18:19.030
we can think of this as
happening in two parts.

00:18:19.030 --> 00:18:21.870
So the first part
is API exposure.

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:25.940
So what can we do to make
the ability for API teams

00:18:25.940 --> 00:18:30.350
to create APIs very
quickly, more efficiently

00:18:30.350 --> 00:18:34.490
to be able to drive the
proliferation of APIs

00:18:34.490 --> 00:18:36.660
within your organization?

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:38.660
And then, we can think
about API consumption.

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:41.660
So what are we doing once
those APIs are up and running

00:18:41.660 --> 00:18:44.390
to actually make it easier for
people to get access to APIs

00:18:44.390 --> 00:18:46.515
and start building those
things, so we can actually

00:18:46.515 --> 00:18:51.030
deliver the real tangible
value to our end users.

00:18:51.030 --> 00:18:55.850
And so again, one
of the things that--

00:18:55.850 --> 00:18:58.520
some of the research we
did leading into this talk

00:18:58.520 --> 00:19:01.070
was to go and ask customers.

00:19:01.070 --> 00:19:04.340
What are the actual KPIs that
you found that you're tracking?

00:19:04.340 --> 00:19:08.510
How do you measure the
value of your API program?

00:19:08.510 --> 00:19:10.370
And so when it comes
to the exposure side--

00:19:10.370 --> 00:19:12.370
taking all the data and
things that you've built

00:19:12.370 --> 00:19:14.540
and getting an API
out more quickly--

00:19:14.540 --> 00:19:16.020
we've heard two things.

00:19:16.020 --> 00:19:17.330
So the first is speed to API.

00:19:17.330 --> 00:19:19.310
So we we've recognized
the need for something,

00:19:19.310 --> 00:19:21.476
and we want to get it up
and running, and available,

00:19:21.476 --> 00:19:24.000
and into the hands of developers
as quickly as possible.

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:25.670
And we don't necessarily
want to do that

00:19:25.670 --> 00:19:28.310
just by throwing more
people at the problem.

00:19:28.310 --> 00:19:31.070
And so we also want to make sure
that the overall cost in order

00:19:31.070 --> 00:19:34.520
to get that API out the door
is as small as possible.

00:19:37.540 --> 00:19:41.520
And the key behind this
is, where an API management

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:45.630
platform can help, is that we
can eliminate a lot of the work

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:47.440
that you would have
to do otherwise.

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:49.020
So how can we use
an API platform

00:19:49.020 --> 00:19:52.890
to allow us to focus in just on
the things that we care about?

00:19:52.890 --> 00:19:56.340
We don't necessarily want to
be experts of API management.

00:19:56.340 --> 00:19:57.930
We want to be
experts of our APIs

00:19:57.930 --> 00:20:01.590
and building the
underlying business logic.

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:03.690
And so we can use an
API management platform

00:20:03.690 --> 00:20:05.490
for things like security.

00:20:05.490 --> 00:20:08.970
So using API management,
can we eliminate the need

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:13.200
for individual security
reviews every time

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:14.340
an API gets published?

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:17.220
Can we do things like
embed all those standards

00:20:17.220 --> 00:20:20.940
that we have, our standard
controls and policies, so that

00:20:20.940 --> 00:20:23.520
anytime an API call comes in,
we're going to automatically

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:26.220
apply the right policies?

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:29.760
Can we use it to tie into our
existing IDPs and our existing

00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:32.220
capability, so that we
don't have to reinvent

00:20:32.220 --> 00:20:34.290
the wheel every time?

00:20:34.290 --> 00:20:37.110
Things like traffic management--
so we start to think about some

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:39.318
of the threats that can
happen, even with data that's

00:20:39.318 --> 00:20:40.870
not necessarily sensitive.

00:20:40.870 --> 00:20:44.430
We have to be aware that
even non-sensitive data can

00:20:44.430 --> 00:20:45.160
be important.

00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:48.030
So for example, we may
have like retailers that

00:20:48.030 --> 00:20:51.250
have store hours, not a
sensitive bit of information,

00:20:51.250 --> 00:20:53.250
but it's something that's
critical for customers

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:55.555
that want to go and look and
see if the store is open.

00:20:55.555 --> 00:20:58.170
So we don't necessarily want
to open that up to the public,

00:20:58.170 --> 00:21:01.530
have a huge wave of traffic flow
in, and take that capability

00:21:01.530 --> 00:21:02.370
down.

00:21:02.370 --> 00:21:05.910
We can use traffic management
and the API management platform

00:21:05.910 --> 00:21:08.130
to be able to solve
those type of use cases

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:11.270
and protect against
those type of threats.

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:12.850
Again, caching will
come into play.

00:21:12.850 --> 00:21:15.090
So can we do things like--

00:21:15.090 --> 00:21:17.220
not just response caching
at the edge-- but can

00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:19.860
we also do things
like partial caching?

00:21:19.860 --> 00:21:22.230
Maybe you look at how
your APIs are structured,

00:21:22.230 --> 00:21:24.540
and you see that it would
actually work better

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:26.100
instead of what you
have today if you

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:27.450
aggregate two or three APIs.

00:21:27.450 --> 00:21:29.310
Maybe you see from
analytics there

00:21:29.310 --> 00:21:31.590
are patterns that are
happening over and over again.

00:21:31.590 --> 00:21:33.910
You want to optimize those APIs.

00:21:33.910 --> 00:21:36.292
But you're scared about
handing back stale data.

00:21:36.292 --> 00:21:38.500
So what we might want to do
is you might want to say,

00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:40.230
well, we're going
to create a mash-up.

00:21:40.230 --> 00:21:42.188
We're going to grab two
or three different APIs

00:21:42.188 --> 00:21:43.620
and put those payloads together.

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:44.995
Two of those things
are cashable.

00:21:44.995 --> 00:21:46.770
We can use partial
caching, and one's not.

00:21:46.770 --> 00:21:48.990
And we can still improve
the overall response time

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:52.590
and performance of that API.

00:21:52.590 --> 00:21:55.500
And then likewise, we get to
things like transformation

00:21:55.500 --> 00:21:56.190
and mediation.

00:21:56.190 --> 00:21:59.331
So if you have an API that was
built for maybe a client server

00:21:59.331 --> 00:22:01.080
app or something that's
running internally

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:03.240
to your organization,
and you want

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:06.059
that to be useful
for a mobile app,

00:22:06.059 --> 00:22:07.850
you can do things like
mobile optimization.

00:22:07.850 --> 00:22:10.080
We can cut the
payload down, trim out

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:12.541
the parts that aren't
needed by the mobile app.

00:22:12.541 --> 00:22:14.040
Same thing for like
data scrubbing--

00:22:14.040 --> 00:22:15.930
so if you've got
an existing API,

00:22:15.930 --> 00:22:18.480
it's got sensitive
customer information on it,

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:20.231
and you want to expose
that to a partner,

00:22:20.231 --> 00:22:22.230
instead of building another
version of that API,

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:24.870
you can use the mediation and
transformation capabilities,

00:22:24.870 --> 00:22:28.260
scrub that out so that your
same API becomes applicable

00:22:28.260 --> 00:22:31.266
and usable by more people.

00:22:31.266 --> 00:22:32.640
And then of course,
we want to be

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:35.280
able to track and get real-time
visibility into our APIs.

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:38.319
If all of a sudden, we see
that things are going bad--

00:22:38.319 --> 00:22:40.860
either errors are coming up, or
the response time is starting

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:42.360
to really trail off--

00:22:42.360 --> 00:22:45.639
we want to be able to
get visibility into that

00:22:45.639 --> 00:22:47.430
so that we can take
the appropriate action.

00:22:50.490 --> 00:22:52.507
From an API
consumption standpoint,

00:22:52.507 --> 00:22:54.840
some of the things that we
heard when we asked customers

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:57.600
were time to Hello World.

00:22:57.600 --> 00:23:00.240
So we build an API portal.

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:03.990
We've let people become aware
of our APIs that we've built.

00:23:03.990 --> 00:23:05.220
They can get access to those.

00:23:05.220 --> 00:23:07.456
How long does it actually
take for them to start

00:23:07.456 --> 00:23:08.580
building something with it?

00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:10.500
Even just a simple Hello World.

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:12.360
So how long before
we see they go

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:14.670
from a registered user
to somebody that actually

00:23:14.670 --> 00:23:17.230
has an app sending traffic in?

00:23:17.230 --> 00:23:21.330
So we want to be able to monitor
and track that data over time

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:23.580
and then improve our
documentation and our outreach

00:23:23.580 --> 00:23:26.082
so that we can improve those.

00:23:26.082 --> 00:23:28.290
We can also look at things
like number of developers.

00:23:28.290 --> 00:23:30.499
So if we're trying
to drive consumption,

00:23:30.499 --> 00:23:32.040
the consumption
comes from developers

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:33.706
that are actually
building apps that are

00:23:33.706 --> 00:23:35.040
going to be using these APIs.

00:23:35.040 --> 00:23:37.410
So we want to be able to
see, are we actually getting

00:23:37.410 --> 00:23:38.530
the adoption that we want?

00:23:38.530 --> 00:23:41.760
Are we reaching the audience
of developers that we're after?

00:23:41.760 --> 00:23:43.510
And then, are they
actually building apps?

00:23:43.510 --> 00:23:45.510
Do we see an uptick
in terms of the number

00:23:45.510 --> 00:23:52.370
of apps that are being built
as we launch more APIs?

00:23:52.370 --> 00:23:55.110
And so how do we do that?

00:23:55.110 --> 00:23:57.810
How can API management
help with that?

00:23:57.810 --> 00:24:00.446
Well, API management
programs should

00:24:00.446 --> 00:24:02.070
be building a very
compelling developer

00:24:02.070 --> 00:24:03.870
experience for your developers.

00:24:03.870 --> 00:24:05.370
You want them to
not only see here's

00:24:05.370 --> 00:24:07.770
the documentation, the
APIs that are available,

00:24:07.770 --> 00:24:10.422
but also see what types
of things are possible.

00:24:10.422 --> 00:24:12.630
We've got a lot of customers
that are building things

00:24:12.630 --> 00:24:14.160
like application showcases.

00:24:14.160 --> 00:24:17.380
So it's very easy for you to go
and say, oh, here's a neat app

00:24:17.380 --> 00:24:19.650
that somebody built.
Hopefully, that triggers ideas

00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:23.580
from other developers so that
they can go build their ideas

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:24.717
with the portal.

00:24:24.717 --> 00:24:26.550
And you want to make
it very easy for people

00:24:26.550 --> 00:24:29.730
to not just see what APIs exist,
but actually try those out

00:24:29.730 --> 00:24:30.715
as well.

00:24:30.715 --> 00:24:32.340
So they can start
using those, and they

00:24:32.340 --> 00:24:36.480
can start experimenting with the
APIs and seeing how they work.

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:38.367
As opposed to
writing a little test

00:24:38.367 --> 00:24:40.200
harness or things like
that, we can actually

00:24:40.200 --> 00:24:43.180
do that right within the
browser with interactive docs.

00:24:43.180 --> 00:24:45.885
You can take an open
API spec, load it in,

00:24:45.885 --> 00:24:47.260
create interactive
documentation,

00:24:47.260 --> 00:24:49.350
and start using that
really right away.

00:24:53.010 --> 00:24:55.340
And if we are able to
do this successfully,

00:24:55.340 --> 00:24:58.070
we've seen some major,
major improvements

00:24:58.070 --> 00:25:00.450
from our customers.

00:25:00.450 --> 00:25:02.990
So here's a good
example from Walgreens.

00:25:02.990 --> 00:25:05.700
So they've been a customer
for a really long time.

00:25:05.700 --> 00:25:07.730
So what they started
out with, they actually

00:25:07.730 --> 00:25:10.760
started out with an API
called the Quick Prints API.

00:25:10.760 --> 00:25:12.410
So somebody at
Walgreens recognized

00:25:12.410 --> 00:25:14.870
that we have all of
these photo printers that

00:25:14.870 --> 00:25:16.400
are sitting in our stores.

00:25:16.400 --> 00:25:18.800
And everybody, some
of you right now,

00:25:18.800 --> 00:25:20.720
are taking photos
with your phones.

00:25:20.720 --> 00:25:23.834
And a lot of times, they
just stay on the phone.

00:25:23.834 --> 00:25:25.500
People aren't printing
them off anymore.

00:25:25.500 --> 00:25:27.470
So what could
Walgreens do in order

00:25:27.470 --> 00:25:30.200
to make it easy for people
to take those photos,

00:25:30.200 --> 00:25:31.700
send them into the store?

00:25:31.700 --> 00:25:33.890
And their answer
to that question

00:25:33.890 --> 00:25:35.564
was, we'll build a
Quick Prints API.

00:25:35.564 --> 00:25:36.480
So we'll build an API.

00:25:36.480 --> 00:25:37.790
We'll put it on our mobile app.

00:25:37.790 --> 00:25:41.660
It will let people take those
pictures, send it to the store.

00:25:41.660 --> 00:25:43.790
Hopefully, we'll get a
little trickle of revenue

00:25:43.790 --> 00:25:48.200
that comes in and justifies
the space in our store.

00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:50.274
And what they found
is, they didn't get

00:25:50.274 --> 00:25:51.440
a little trickle of revenue.

00:25:51.440 --> 00:25:55.010
They got a tremendous boost
in terms of those users.

00:25:55.010 --> 00:25:59.420
So the users that were actually
interacting with Walgreens

00:25:59.420 --> 00:26:03.050
on their mobile channel, on
their store channel, the brick

00:26:03.050 --> 00:26:06.759
and mortar, and online
were spending about 6x

00:26:06.759 --> 00:26:08.300
what anybody else
was, the folks that

00:26:08.300 --> 00:26:09.650
would just come into the store.

00:26:09.650 --> 00:26:10.910
Because these customers
were coming in

00:26:10.910 --> 00:26:13.010
to pick up their photo, and
then they were buying shampoo.

00:26:13.010 --> 00:26:13.760
They were buying toothpaste.

00:26:13.760 --> 00:26:16.460
They were buying whatever else
that they needed at Walgreens.

00:26:16.460 --> 00:26:21.200
And it ended up being a
real eye opener for them.

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:24.050
Once they realized that, they
said we should open this up.

00:26:24.050 --> 00:26:26.720
We should take this API, and
we should have let anybody

00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:28.580
use our Quick Prints API.

00:26:28.580 --> 00:26:30.200
And so they started
to offer this

00:26:30.200 --> 00:26:32.270
to lots of other
mobile apps that

00:26:32.270 --> 00:26:34.580
are out there that allow
you to take photos and print

00:26:34.580 --> 00:26:35.840
photos off.

00:26:35.840 --> 00:26:38.210
And so now, they're
up to about 300

00:26:38.210 --> 00:26:42.244
partners that are building APIs
on top of this Quick Prints.

00:26:42.244 --> 00:26:44.660
And that's 300 partners that
are constantly sending people

00:26:44.660 --> 00:26:47.210
into the stores to come in,
to pick up those photos,

00:26:47.210 --> 00:26:48.820
and buy other
things at Walgreens.

00:26:48.820 --> 00:26:50.810
And it just made a
tremendous impact.

00:26:50.810 --> 00:26:54.040
And so when I think of this
successful API program,

00:26:54.040 --> 00:26:56.730
I view Walgreens as
really the archetype

00:26:56.730 --> 00:26:58.580
that I feel like
a lot of companies

00:26:58.580 --> 00:27:01.620
should strive for because it's
not just necessarily an IT

00:27:01.620 --> 00:27:02.120
problem.

00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:04.340
It's really something
that can deliver

00:27:04.340 --> 00:27:06.764
tremendous overall
business value.

00:27:06.764 --> 00:27:10.978
[MUSIC PLAYING]

